File permissions|Unix mode bits grant read, write and execute rights separately to the owner, group and everyone else.|For regular files, read exposes bytes, write changes bytes and execute permits execution. For directories, read lists names, execute traverses paths and write changes directory entries. Mode 640 grants owner read/write and group read; every parent directory still needs search permission.|stat -c '%A %a %U %G %n' /etc/hosts
Ownership|Each file has a numeric owner UID and group GID; names are lookups for those numbers.|The kernel compares the process credentials with ownership and permission rules. chown changes ownership, while chmod changes mode bits. Supplementary groups and ACLs can affect access even when the displayed owner differs.|id
Symbolic links|A symbolic link is a filesystem object containing another pathname.|The kernel follows the target during pathname resolution. Relative targets resolve from the link's directory, and targets can cross filesystems or disappear. Inspect the resolved path when a release link stops working.|readlink -f /etc/localtime
Hard links|Hard links are multiple directory entries referring to the same inode on one filesystem.|They share content and metadata. Removing one name decreases the link count; storage is reclaimed only when no links or open references remain. A hard link is not a second independent backup.|ls -li /etc/hosts
Pipelines|A shell pipeline connects one program's standard output to another program's standard input.|The pipe buffers bytes and applies backpressure when the reader is slower. Standard error stays separate unless redirected. In Bash, pipefail makes a failing earlier stage affect pipeline status, which matters in automation.|set -o pipefail
Quoting|Shell quoting controls which expansions and special characters are interpreted by the shell.|Double quotes allow variable expansion while preventing word splitting and globbing of the result. Single quotes preserve literal characters. Pass untrusted values as arguments rather than building shell code and evaluating it.|printf '%s\n' "$HOME"
Processes|A process is a running program with credentials, memory mappings, file descriptors and scheduling state.|The kernel assigns a PID within a PID namespace. Child processes inherit selected state from their parent, and PIDs can be reused after exit. Check command, owner and start time before acting on a PID.|ps -eo pid,ppid,user,stat,etime,comm
Signals|Signals notify a process of events such as termination requests or child exits.|SIGTERM can be handled for cleanup; SIGKILL cannot be caught. Graceful shutdown should stop new work, drain active work and exit within a deadline. A signal-based exit status alone does not establish why the signal was sent.|kill -l
Disk blocks|Filesystems allocate storage in blocks, and df reports allocation for a mounted filesystem.|du totals file usage visible through directory traversal. Open deleted files, snapshots, reserved space and mount boundaries can explain differences between the two. Inspect these causes before deleting data to chase a discrepancy.|df -hT
Inodes|An inode stores file metadata and references to its data, separately from the filename in a directory.|Some filesystems have a bounded inode supply. Millions of tiny files can consume it while byte capacity remains. Check both block and inode utilization when creating files fails with no space left.|df -i
Open file limits|A file descriptor is a process-local handle for an open file, socket, pipe or similar resource.|The soft descriptor limit constrains normal allocation; the hard limit bounds unprivileged increases. Repeatedly rising descriptor counts may indicate a leak. Raising the limit without measuring demand can postpone rather than fix failure.|ulimit -n
Load average|Linux load average summarizes runnable tasks and tasks in uninterruptible sleep over time.|The three values represent approximately one, five and fifteen minutes. Compare them with CPU count, task states and I/O latency. High load with idle CPU often requires investigating blocked I/O rather than adding cores.|uptime
Memory cache|The page cache keeps recently used file data in RAM to avoid repeated storage access.|Much cached memory is reclaimable when applications need it. Available memory estimates usable headroom more meaningfully than free memory alone. Reclaim pressure, swap activity and latency distinguish useful caching from shortage.|free -h
OOM evidence|An out-of-memory kill occurs when the kernel or a constrained memory cgroup cannot satisfy memory demand under its policy.|A cgroup can exhaust its own limit while the host has spare RAM. Confirm an OOM event through kernel logs or cgroup memory.events, then compare limits and workload growth. Exit 137 also occurs after other SIGKILL events.|journalctl -k --since '-15 min'
Socket inspection|A socket is an endpoint for network or local interprocess communication.|Listening sockets accept new connections; established sockets carry active conversations. A listener bound to 127.0.0.1 is not exposed on the host's external interface. Inspect protocol, address and port before changing network rules.|ss -lntp
Name resolution|Linux name-service resolution maps names to records using configured sources such as local files and DNS.|NSS configuration controls lookup order for applications using the system resolver. A direct DNS query does not test local hosts entries or every application's private cache. Compare getent results with DNS responses and application behavior.|getent hosts localhost
Mount options|A mount attaches a filesystem view to a directory and applies options to that view.|Read-only, noexec, nosuid and nodev constrain different operations. File mode bits cannot override a read-only mount. A filesystem unexpectedly remounted read-only may signal storage errors that require investigation before restoring writes.|findmnt -o TARGET,SOURCE,FSTYPE,OPTIONS
Deleted executables|A process can retain mappings to executable data after its pathname has been removed or replaced.|Replacing a package on disk does not switch an existing process to the new code. Controlled restarts are often needed after updates. Inspect mapped executable versions and service start times when verifying patch completion.|readlink /proc/1/exe
Zombies|A zombie is an exited child process whose parent has not yet collected its exit status.|It no longer runs application code but retains a process-table entry. The parent should call a wait operation or otherwise arrange child reaping. Killing the zombie again is ineffective; investigate the parent's lifecycle handling.|ps -eo pid,ppid,stat,comm
System calls|A system call is a controlled request from user space to the kernel for services such as file I/O or memory mapping.|Tracing shows call arguments, return codes and timing, making permission failures and blocking operations visible. It can add overhead and reveal sensitive arguments, so trace a narrow process and time window.|strace -e trace=file /usr/bin/true
Scheduling priority|The normal Linux scheduler shares CPU among runnable tasks using scheduling weights and other controls.|Nice values influence relative weight; a larger nice value usually means less preference. They do not impose a hard CPU ceiling or guarantee latency. Cgroup bandwidth limits and placement solve different problems.|ps -eo pid,ni,pri,comm
Swap pressure|Swap provides backing storage for selected memory pages when the kernel moves them out of RAM.|Configured swap is not itself a fault. Sustained swap-in and swap-out combined with reclaim stalls and latency suggests working-set pressure. Disabling swap under pressure can instead force allocation failures or OOM kills.|vmstat 1 5
Capabilities|Linux capabilities divide traditional root privileges into more specific permissions.|A process may receive narrowly scoped powers such as binding privileged ports. Effective, permitted and other capability sets determine what can be exercised or inherited. Some capabilities remain very powerful, so combine them with other isolation controls.|getcap /usr/bin/ping
ACL masks|POSIX ACLs extend the owner/group/other model with named users and groups.|The ACL mask caps the effective rights of named users and the group class. An entry may display write permission while its effective rights exclude write. Default directory ACLs influence new entries but do not retroactively update existing files.|getfacl /etc/hosts
Atomic replacement|Atomic replacement publishes a complete new file by renaming it over the old directory entry on the same filesystem.|Readers opening the path see an old or new version rather than a partly written file. Existing open descriptors may still read the old inode. Crash durability additionally needs appropriate file and directory synchronization; atomic visibility alone is insufficient.|
Namespaces|Namespaces give processes separate views of resources such as PIDs, mounts, networks and hostnames.|They control visibility and identity boundaries. Two processes in different network namespaces can have separate routing tables while still sharing CPU and the kernel. Cgroups provide resource accounting and limits rather than namespace visibility.|lsns
Cgroup throttling|Control groups account for and constrain resource use by groups of processes.|A CPU quota limits runnable time during a scheduling period. A group can be throttled even when other host cores are idle. Compare throttling counters, quotas and latency before raising a limit or changing concurrency.|cat /sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.stat
Filesystem recovery|Filesystem recovery restores metadata consistency after damage or an unclean failure.|Tools and procedures depend on the filesystem. Stop uncontrolled writes, preserve recoverable data and use the supported offline or online process. Repairing an actively writable filesystem with an offline repair tool can worsen corruption.|lsblk -f
Clock selection|Wall clocks represent calendar time; monotonic clocks measure elapsed time within a system's clock domain.|Clock synchronization or administrative changes can adjust wall time. Deadlines and durations should use an appropriate monotonic source, while distributed logs need synchronized wall timestamps and correlation IDs. Monotonic timestamps from different hosts are not directly comparable.|timedatectl status
Kernel versus user space|User space runs ordinary applications; the kernel mediates privileged access to hardware and shared resources.|Applications request kernel services through system calls. Device, filesystem and kernel failures can affect unrelated applications simultaneously. Shared symptoms and kernel logs help distinguish infrastructure faults from a single application's logic.|uname -r
